  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Activity under preparation Supporting Clean Cooking Solutions in Southern Africa and Indian Ocean Regions</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">&lt;div class=&quot;quill-better-table-wrapper&quot;&gt;&lt;table class=&quot;quill-better-table&quot;&gt;&lt;colgroup&gt;&lt;col width=&quot;100&quot;&gt;&lt;/colgroup&gt;&lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr data-row=&quot;1&quot;&gt;&lt;td data-row=&quot;1&quot; rowspan=&quot;1&quot; colspan=&quot;1&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;qlbt-cell-line&quot; data-row=&quot;1&quot; data-cell=&quot;1&quot; data-rowspan=&quot;1&quot; data-colspan=&quot;1&quot;&gt;Sub-Saharan Africa (SSA) is the only region in the World where clean cooking access has not kept pace with population growth. Under today&#xbf;s policies and investments, most African countries are not expected to reach full clean cooking access even in the 2050s. As such, global efforts to increase clean cooking access need to target SSA with greater intensity to reach Sustainable Development Goal 7. The International Energy Agency (IEA) estimates the upfront investment costs to be USD 4 billion annually from now to 2030 to reach universal access to clean cooking in SSA. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;qlbt-cell-line&quot; data-row=&quot;1&quot; data-cell=&quot;1&quot; data-rowspan=&quot;1&quot; data-colspan=&quot;1&quot;&gt;Access to clean cooking in Southern Africa and Indian Ocean Region varies from less than 10% of the population in Malawi, Mozambique, Madagascar, Zambia, Comoros, to 30-50% in Angola, Lesotho, Mozambique, Namibia, Zimbabwe, while Botswana reaches 66%, and South Africa, Seychelles and Mauritius more than 90%, according to the IEA. Solid biomass remains the primary source of energy for cooking for around 150 million persons of the region.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;qlbt-cell-line&quot; data-row=&quot;1&quot; data-cell=&quot;1&quot; data-rowspan=&quot;1&quot; data-colspan=&quot;1&quot;&gt;The Overall Objective&lt;strong&gt; &lt;/strong&gt;of this action is to increase the access to affordable, reliable and clean cooking solutions in Southern Africa and Indian Ocean Region. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;qlbt-cell-line&quot; data-row=&quot;1&quot; data-cell=&quot;1&quot; data-rowspan=&quot;1&quot; data-colspan=&quot;1&quot;&gt;The Specific Objectives&lt;strong&gt; &lt;/strong&gt;of this action are to: &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr data-row=&quot;2&quot;&gt;&lt;td data-row=&quot;2&quot; rowspan=&quot;1&quot; colspan=&quot;1&quot;&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;qlbt-cell-line&quot; data-row=&quot;2&quot; data-cell=&quot;1&quot; data-rowspan=&quot;1&quot; data-colspan=&quot;1&quot;&gt;1. Foster clean cooking business development through coaching, financial partnership brokering and innovative financial solutions for SMEs (manufacturers, distributors and importers of higher tiers clean cooking solutions), including women-led SMEs, and through training of national finance institutions. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p class=&quot;qlbt-cell-line&quot; data-row=&quot;2&quot; data-cell=&quot;1&quot; data-rowspan=&quot;1&quot; data-colspan=&quot;1&quot;&gt;2. Contribute to a more supportive regulatory and market framework to the uptake and trade of clean cooking solutions in the region.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</narrative>
  </description>
